  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "A particle moves in a straight line so that after $t$ seconds it is $p(t)={t}^{3}-11t+9$ feet from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position when $t=3$ seconds?",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position when $t=3$ seconds is equal to ${p}^{\u2032}(3).$\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-11t+9) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting $t=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{\u2032}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-11 \\\\  & =27-11 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the particle's position when $t=3$ seconds is $16$ feet per second.",
